{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BJYrfDH7wCmKduVBzwSVWP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can reproduce it. gtimeout returns -1 (signal-killed) while our binary returns 129. The -1 means gtimeout itself was killed by a signal.\n\nSo the issue is: when sh kills itself with HUP in a child process started by gtimeout, gtimeout also gets HUP somehow.\n\nWhy would this happen? Let me think...\n\nGNU timeout does `setpgid(0, 0)` on the child, creating a new process group. When the child sh does `kill -HUP $$`, it sends HUP to itself (its own PID). \n\nsh dies from HUP, and gtimeout waits for the child and sees it was killed by SIGHUP—so it should exit 129. But gtimeout is exiting with -1, meaning it was killed by a signal itself. I think GNU timeout installs signal handlers to forward signals to the child process group, and when the child dies, something causes gtimeout to also receive SIGHUP.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering this. The child sh is in a different process group, so its death shouldn't propagate HUP back to gtimeout. Maybe the issue is with how the child sends the signal—if sh does `kill -HUP $$` and the process group setup makes the PID and PGID the same, that could be relevant. Let me check what signal gtimeout is actually receiving.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":839,"cache_read_input_tokens":40623,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":839},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8854d2c-587c-4d04-b6c4-9a7d2677a20e","uuid":"592ffa2d-f2f4-466c-8d13-a4220a7137ea"}
