{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01P7GNTfvkQab23M48obbV5q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! For every signal, gtimeout itself gets killed by the same signal. This tells me that GNU timeout forwards/re-raises signals. When the child kills itself, somehow the signal reaches gtimeout too.\n\nI think what's happening is: GNU timeout monitors the child. When the child dies from a signal, GNU timeout re-raises that signal on itself. This is the GNU behavior for \"preserving\" the signal information for the parent process.\n\nLooking at the GNU timeout source code behavior: when a child dies from a signal, GNU timeout sends that signal to itself so that the parent (the test runner in our case) can see the correct signal death rather than just an exit code. \n\nThis is standard practice for wrapper programs. So our timeout implementation should actually re-raise the signal on itself when the child is killed by a signal, rather than just exiting with code 128+signum. That way the parent process sees the actual signal death, which is what GNU timeout does. The R3.3 requirement about exiting with 128+signum matches what the shell would show, but there's a distinction between the exit code and how the process actually terminates.\n\nThe fix is to use signal re-raising instead of a clean exit, which matches the existing pattern already in the code for SIGKILL.\n\nNow I need to reconsider the logic more carefully: when the timeout signal kills the command, I should return 124 (or 128+signum with --preserve-status), but when a different signal kills it, I need to re-raise that signal on ourselves. For the --preserve-status case with the timeout's own signal, I'm not sure yet whether re-raising is also needed or if returning the exit code is sufficient.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1403,"cache_read_input_tokens":42583,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1403},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8854d2c-587c-4d04-b6c4-9a7d2677a20e","uuid":"1222af87-e43b-4626-adde-9fdbf3dd15b4"}
