y whether the runtime is intercepting the specific signal we're trying to re-raise. in exit code 1 rather than a signal death. I need to reset the signal handler to its default disposition with `signal.Reset` and then re-raise it, so the process actually terminates from the signal itself. Looking at Go's signal handling, most signals are either ignored by default or cause a crash, but SIGTERM specifically gets handled by the runtime to trigger a graceful shutdown—so I need to undo that with Reset before calling `syscall.Kill` to get the actual signal termination behavior.\n\nSetting up syscall handlers... Actually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. The key insight is that Go's signal handling makes it tricky to reset to the default handler, so I need to use the raw syscall approach with `sigaction` to set the signal back to `SIG_DFL` before raising it to myself. I need to figure out how to properly re-raise a signal in Go so the process actually dies from it rather than exiting cleanly. The issue is that Go's runtime intercepts certain signals like SIGTERM and converts them to regular exits, so I can't just call `syscall.Kill` directly. I'm exploring whether using `signal.Notify` to reset the handler and then re-raise would work, or if I need to look at how other Go programs handle this scenario. Go's `os/signal` package resets the runtime's signal handling, but that gives a clean exit rather than a true signal death. To get the OS-level default behavior, I need to use raw syscalls to set the signal disposition back to SIG_DFL. I'm exploring different approaches—trying `sigaction` with the syscall package, considering the unix package from golang.org/x/sys, and testing the simplest possible implementation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":765,"cache_read_input_tokens":45451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":765},"output_toke