{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LJebnVaQ2kjZpRg19cNNxT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, HUP works now (both -1 and signaled=true). But USR1, USR2, ABRT, and TERM still don't match. The issue is that our binary still shows clean exit (signaled=false) for those signals.\n\nWait, the test used `bin/timeout` which is the old binary! I need to rebuild it. Let me check...\n\nActually, I did `go build -o bin/timeout ./cmd/timeout/` and it succeeded. But the test is running `bin/timeout` which should be the new binary. Let me check if the path is correct.\n \nThe working directory might be throwing things off. Let me rebuild and test again. Looking at the results more carefully, HUP is working but the others aren't—the issue is likely that Go's runtime handles USR1, USR2, ABRT, and TERM differently than HUP. I think `signal.Reset()` only resets Go-level handling, while the `sigaction` syscall sets the OS-level handler to SIG_DFL, but Go's runtime might reinstall its own handler before the kill signal takes effect.\n\nFor SIGUSR1 specifically, Go ignores it by default since it's not one of the signals like SIGTERM, SIGINT, SIGHUP, or SIGQUIT that trigger program exit. Let me check Go's actual signal behavior documentation to understand which signals cause termination versus which ones are ignored.\n\nActually, I realize the issue: Go's runtime installs its own signal handlers for nearly all signals during initialization, so when I call `sigaction` to set SIG_DFL, I'm replacing Go's handler. But Go's runtime might not re-install its handler afterward, which means the signal should then follow the OS default behavior. The problem is that Go's signal dispatching mechanism intercepts signals before they reach the OS default handler, so setting SIG_DFL might not have the effect I expect.\n\nLet me check if the binary was actually rebuilt before running the test, since the test uses a relative path `\"bin/timeout\"` that depends on the current working directory when `go run` executes.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":412,"cache_read_input_tokens":62574,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":412},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8854d2c-587c-4d04-b6c4-9a7d2677a20e","uuid":"6dbcb069-c226-4866-9407-dc9cc9c6a31e"}
