{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NsDnVf4hDJcLe3QDuA8Esv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The sigaction call succeeds (errno=0), but the process is \"still alive\" after `syscall.Kill(os.Getpid(), sig)`. This means Go's runtime is catching the signal even after we set SIG_DFL via sigaction.\n\nI think the issue is that Go's runtime runs its signal handling on a separate OS thread (the signal handler thread), and `sigaction` only affects the calling thread's signal mask on some systems. But actually, `sigaction` sets the signal disposition globally for the process, not per-thread. So the handler should be SIG_DFL.\n \nThe real problem is that Go's runtime is likely re-installing its own signal handlers after we call sigaction. During initialization, the runtime sets up handlers for all signals, and when one arrives, its handler intercepts it and routes it to the appropriate goroutine instead of letting the default action execute. Our sigaction call changes the disposition, but the runtime's signal handling mechanism is still in place and takes precedence.\n\nI should try using RawSyscall instead of Syscall—RawSyscall bypasses the runtime's notification system entirely, which might prevent the runtime from interfering with our signal handler setup.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":722,"cache_read_input_tokens":68498,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":722},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8854d2c-587c-4d04-b6c4-9a7d2677a20e","uuid":"f2ba8371-3773-4bb5-87e3-465f3716e320"}
