{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WXh6jd8d74UFurxSxRwB8V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"It works for ALL signals! The key was adding the `sigprocmask(SIG_UNBLOCK, ...)` call before `kill()`. Go's runtime blocks these signals on goroutine threads, so we need to explicitly unblock them.\n\nThe working approach:\n1. `signal.Reset(sig)` - tell Go's signal package to stop handling\n2. `runtime.LockOSThread()` - pin to OS thread\n3. `RawSyscall(46, sig, &sa{SIG_DFL}, 0)` - set handler to SIG_DFL via __sigaction\n\n\n4. `RawSyscall(48, SIG_UNBLOCK, &mask, 0)` - unblock signal on this thread via sigprocmask\n5. `RawSyscall(SYS_KILL, pid, sig, 0)` - send signal to self\n\nNo CGo needed! Now I'm updating the timeout implementation, but I'm realizing there's a potential issue with signal numbers above 31 on macOS—the platform only supports signals 1-31, so the bitmask approach using `1 << (sig-1)` should handle this range correctly.\n\nI should add a safety check for edge cases and make sure SIGKILL is handled properly since it can't be caught or blocked anyway. Now I need to update the `reraiseSignal` function in `main.go`, fix the `unsafe` import, and add `runtime` to the imports.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1398,"cache_read_input_tokens":82545,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1398},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8854d2c-587c-4d04-b6c4-9a7d2677a20e","uuid":"244b47a7-732d-4c9f-a380-3303a88b1cd1"}
