le.\n\nThe real issue is that Go's signal handler might panic or exit cleanly when it receives the signal rather than returning control to me. Looking at how Go handles signals, for things like SIGTERM it just exits the process cleanly, and for signals nobody's called `signal.Notify` on after a `signal.Reset`, the runtime falls back to its default behavior.\n\nWhen I call `signal.Reset(sig)`, Go reinstalls its own handler which causes a clean exit for TERM/HUP/INT instead of letting the signal actually kill the process. Even if I then call `sigaction(SIG_DFL)`, Go's sysmon thread might reinstall its handler before my `kill` call lands.\n\nThe CGo approach works better because the C code executes atomically from Go's perspective—the sysmon thread can't interrupt the C code on the same OS thread, and the sigaction plus raise sequence is fast enough that it usually completes before sysmon checks again. But actually, the pure Go approach also uses `LockOSThread` and `RawSyscall`, which should be equally fast and avoid Go runtime checkpoints. So maybe the real issue is that Go's signal handler intercepts the signal on delivery before SIG_DFL can handle it—if sysmon reinstalls Go's handler between my sigaction and kill calls, the signal gets routed to Go's handler instead of terminating the process.\n\nThis race condition would explain the inconsistency. Let me try using `syscall.Exec` to spawn a helper process that sends the signal, though I need to think through whether the PID gets preserved correctly during exec...\n\nActually, after exec the process image is replaced but the PID stays the same, so `/bin/kill` would send the signal to itself and die from it, which gives us the signal death status we need. The catch is that exec wipes out everything—all goroutines and file descriptors—so once the process becomes `/bin/kill` and dies from the signal, it's truly gone with the right exit code. I'm realizing the shell approach might actually work—if I use `exec /bin/sh -c \"kill -USR1 $$\"`, the shell expands `$$` to its own PID before executing, then kills itself with that signal, which should propagate the signal death to the parent process.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":280,"cache_read_input_tokens":88802,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":280},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8854d2c-587c-4d04-b6c4-9a7d2677a20e","uuid":"766bf0e1-b71c-45ac-a747-20d14b806558"}
